// Annex C.7 excludes DIGITAL events ("No digital behavior or events are
// supported in Verilog-A"), and C.2 lists "a set of events to control when
// certain code is simulated" among the features the subset provides. §5.10
// enumerates three kinds of ANALOG event — global (5.10.2), monitored (5.10.3)
// and named (5.10.4) — so a named event declared and triggered inside the
// analog block is on the Verilog-A side of the C.7 line, and the digital edge
// events that are on the other side are 21_digital_event_control_rejected.va.
//
// This file replaces 19_digital_event_trigger.va, which asserted the opposite:
// it demanded a diagnostic for `event tick; -> tick;` and pinned it on the
// substring "DiagnosticsReported", a torture.zig phase label that every rejected
// fixture in the suite matches and that names no rule. A conforming compiler
// accepts this source, so as a `//! reject` the fixture was inverted. It also
// contradicted ch05_analog_behavior/named_event_unsupported.va, which argues the
// same reading from Clause 5; the two now agree.
//
// §5.10.4: "Named events can be triggered from always and initial blocks, or
// from an analog event statement", and the clause closes with an example that
// does exactly what this fixture does — triggers from an analog event statement
// and detects in the same analog block:
//
//     event ana_event;
//     ...
//     analog begin
//        @(timer(1n)) -> ana_event;
//        @(ana_event) $display("Event: ana_event detected in analog");
//        @(dig_event) $display("Event: dig_event detected in analog");
//     end
//
// introduced as "how an event (ana_event) can be generated from an analog event
// statement, not only to trigger an event-controlled statement in the analog
// block, but also in an always statement". So the clause itself states the
// analog-block detection fires; the 1.0 below is that, not an inference from
// statement order alone. This fixture swaps `timer(1n)` for initial_step
// (§5.10.2, Table 5-1: 1 at the DC operating point) so it needs only the one dc
// timepoint.
//
// §5.10.5, "Digital events in analog behavior", lists named events among the
// digital events analog behavior "can be made sensitive to" — and 5.10.5 is
// what 21_digital_event_control_rejected.va cites to reject `posedge`. The two
// readings do not conflict: 5.10.5's next sentence points back at the SAME
// example, at the `dig_event` line, whose trigger is `initial #10 -> dig_event;`
// in a digital process. What 5.10.5 describes is an event crossing FROM the
// digital side, which is the mixed-signal feature C.7 drops. `ana_event` —
// declared, triggered and detected entirely inside the analog block, the shape
// used here — is the §5.10.4 case and never touches a digital process. Trigger
// this `tick` from an `initial` block instead and the fixture would be a 5.10.5
// case and would belong with 21.
//
// Statements in an analog block run in order, so the trigger has happened by the
// time the detection statement is reached and `seen` is 1.0. §3.2.1 starts a real
// at 0.0, so a compiler that treats `@(tick)` as a guard that is never true reads
// 0.0 — the two outcomes are distinguishable.
//
//! lrm C.2
//! lrm C.7
//! lrm 5.10.4
`include "check.vh"
module annex_c_named_event_in_subset(p);
    inout p;
    electrical p;
    real seen;
    event tick;

    analog begin
        @(initial_step) -> tick;
        @(tick) seen = 1.0;
        `CHECKX("a named event triggered in the analog block is detected in it", seen, 1.0);
        I(p) <+ 1m * seen;
    end
endmodule
